<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raymond IV of Toulouse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1041–1105</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Raymond of Saint-Gilles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raymond of Saint-Gilles, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, was one of the leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. After the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> he turned to the Lebanese coast and from 1102 besieged Tripoli from a castle he built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pilgrim">Mount Pilgrim</a>. He died in 1105, and his son Bertrand took the city in 1109, founding the County of Tripoli.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse.jpg" alt="Raymond IV of Toulouse" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles François Toustain, René Prosper Tassin, Jean Baptiste Baussonnet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raymond_St_Gilles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raymond of Saint-Gilles was a son of Pons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and Almodis de La Marche. He received Saint-Gilles with the title of count from his father. In 1094 he displaced his niece Philippa, Duchess of Aquitaine, daughter of his brother William IV, from inheriting Toulouse, and the same year the margravial title of Provence passed to him on the death of William Bertrand. He married three times: first the daughter of Godfrey I, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, then Matilda, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-i-of-sicily">Roger I of Sicily</a>, and in 1094 Elvira, an illegitimate daughter of Alfonso VI of León. He was twice excommunicated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a>, in 1076 and 1078, for marrying within forbidden degrees of kinship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Described as deeply religious, Raymond was one of the first to take the cross for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. The oldest and richest of the leaders, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> at the end of October 1096 with a large army and the papal legate Adhemar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-puy-en-velay">Le Puy</a>. He alone of the leaders refused to swear an oath of fealty to the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>, offering an oath of friendship instead, because he thought fealty conflicted with his vow to serve God. His army reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in October 1097, and the city fell in June 1098 after a long siege. Raymond took the palace of the emir Yaghi-Siyan, and a struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-i-of-antioch">Bohemond of Taranto</a> over possession of Antioch followed.</p><p>After Adhemar died Raymond became the leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> and led it towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, besieging Arqa in 1099 in an attempt to carve out a territory around Tripoli. Jerusalem was captured on 15 July 1099. Raymond refused the crown of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, saying that he shuddered to think of being called its king, but he held the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and gave it up only with difficulty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a>. He was defeated at the Battle of Mersivan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the crusade of 1101.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1102 Raymond sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, where Tancred imprisoned him until he promised not to conquer land between Antioch and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>. He broke the promise at once by capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartus">Tartus</a> and began building a castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pilgrim">Mons Peregrinus</a>, Pilgrim's Mountain, to support his siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>. In September 1104 the qadi of Tripoli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-mulk-ibn-ammar">Fakhr al-Mulk ibn Ammar</a>, attacked the castle and set a wing on fire. Raymond escaped across a rooftop but was badly burned, and he died on 28 February 1105 before Tripoli was taken.</p><p>Tripoli was captured in 1109 and the County of Tripoli was founded, and it stayed with the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> through the 12th century. Raymond seems to have been driven by both religious and material motives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raymond IV of Toulouse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Raymond IV</b>, also known as Raymond of Saint-Gilles, took the cross in 1095 and led probably the largest contingent of the First Crusade, accompanied by the papal legate Adhemar of Le Puy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Bohemond, <b>Raymond IV of Toulouse</b> and Godfrey of Bouillon took positions at different gates for a siege of nine months.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-21">21 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1102 <b>Raymond of Saint-Gilles</b> besieged Tripoli from a castle he built on Mount Pilgrim.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-ammar-of-tripoli">Banu Ammar of Tripoli</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Raymond of Saint-Gilles</b>, count of Toulouse, besieged Tripoli from 1102, building the castle of Mount Pilgrim outside the city, but died in 1105.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tripoli">County of Tripoli</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Citadel of Tripoli, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.43,35.84&amp;year=1082" aria-label="Citadel of Tripoli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Raymond IV of Toulouse"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5412 4611h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5412 4611h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.43,35.84&amp;year=1082">What was here in 1082? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
